I like the idea of musket-wielding enemies.  If a musket can be held
and used by anything with hands, then even the common or garden gnome
could be a threat, as if he held a non-reflectable wand of death.

However, as described here, the arquebus won't be very useful in
practice.  It requires specialist (and probably rare) ammunition, it is
inaccurate, takes ages to reload, misfires, and - I love this part - it
might just decide to kill its user?  No thanks, it's easy to deal lots
of damage via conventional methods already.

A possible strategy:

A mind flayer is coming towards the player.  The PC has no means to
avoid its brain eating attacks.  Instead, he tosses a cursed arquebus
at it.  Bonk, 2 damage.  Ooh, thinks the mind flayer, which picks it
up, wields it, points it at the player, and is blown to bits in a
rather hilarious fashion.

Thinking about it, surely arquebuses would need gunpowder to work?  If
that's the case, and musketeers showed up in the castle, as proposed,
then there should be a few barrel-fuls of gunpowder just lying around
there as well.  It might be fun to zap a wand of fire at them and watch
the fireworks.

On second thought, the only way to make the arquebus really useful is
to figure out a way to load it with a rubber chicken.  Imagine *that*
missile hurtling towards you.  And imagine the trice-loaded arquebus
exploding in battle, and turning all adjacent monsters into stone.  :)



